{"id":"977f9df6-b35a-4694-b39a-871333480a36","object":"chat.completion","created":1790552589,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"facts\":[{\"statement\":\"The document title is 'Arinimal an Enigma? – by M.K Raina'.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"The document URL is https://mkraina.com/arinimal-an-enigma-by-m-k-raina/.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Kamil writes that Mahmud Gaami is a historical fact and Arinimaal a conceived character.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"The article states that all this is to show that the poem 'åríní rang gom shràvan híyè' is that of Mehmud Gaami and not that of Arinimaal.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"There are numerous instances to show that verses of various poets have been recorded differently at different places.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Amin Kamil accepts this in Chapter 4 of his book 'Kulyat Habba Khatoon'.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"On the controversy regarding this poem, Azad says that we often find such lamentation in the verses of Arinimaal.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Azad says that this poem has been published in the name of Mehmud Gaami, but the circumstantial evidence and peculiarity of the ode tell us that it reflects the emotions of Mrs. Bhawani Das.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"T.N.Kaul in his book 'Gems of Kashmiri Literature' writes that it will not be out of place to mention here that some cussed and overzealous literary critics have wrongly attributed one of Arinimaal's most pathetic ditties – 'åríní rang gom shràvan híyè', to her contemporary, Mahmud Gami (1765-1855).\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"T.N.Kaul further writes that a 90 year old descendant of the Kachru family told Kaul in an exclusive interview in Srinagar in 1987 that Arinimaal had herself also recorded a large number of her poems while she remained separated from her beloved at Palhalan village.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"T.N.Kaul further writes that after Arinimaal's death, these creations were handed over to the old man's ancestors who kept them in safe custody.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"T.N.Kaul further writes that in view of the atrocities perpetrated by the Afghans in the closing years of their rule on the civilian population and the consequent risk of damage to the invaluable poems, the Kachrus were obliged to deposit this treasure in a 'Chah' (dry well) near the Hari Parbat hill.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Shashi Shekhar Toshakhani, in his book 'Kashmiri Sahitya ka Itihas', writes that this is one of the most popular Kashmiri songs, because of its melody and compassion.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Toshakhani writes that it is so popular that Mehmud Gaami, a famous poet, has also used its few lines in one of his poems.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Toshakhani writes that because of this, some critics like Ghulam Nabi Khayal consider it to be originally of Mehmud Gaami.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Toshakhani writes that by detailed analysis and comparison, Autar Krishen Rehbar has proved that the basic lines are that of Arinimaal only.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Toshakhani writes that Rehbar has clearly shown that these are two different poems, only its refrain, 'åríní rang gom shràvan híyè', is common in both.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"There are several instances where verse of one poet has wrongly (may be inadvertently) been attributed to other.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Commenting on the confusion regarding year of birth of Mehmud Gaami, Naji Munawar writes that after going through the manuscripts titled 'Yakh Hakayath' and 'Ponpuri' we can come to the conclusion that either the Mehmud's era has wrongly been calculated ….. or, there has been another poet named Mehmud, whose verse has got messed up with that of Mehmud Gaami, like, some of Maqbool Amritsari's poetry was being printed in the name of Maqbool Kralawari.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Regarding 'kävi víhínúm aríníní …' poem, Kamil says that this is said to have been attributed to Arinimaal only because it contained the word 'Arini'.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Kamil says that it is clear that this poem was not attached to her as late as 1946.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Azad, on the other hand, assumes the poem to be that of Munshi Bhawani Das.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"In another twist to the issue, Kamil feels pleasure in putting this poem in the basket of Habba Khatoon.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Kamil says that this poem is also said to be that of Habba Khatoon, and genuinely so, because she has, during her royal times, composed some poems with twisted words (lafzû gyúnd kåríth).\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Kamil says that this poem also contains the same twist.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Kamil does not mention who told him so and with what proof.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Another point of contention has been the verse 'tarvûní margû tû vasvûní bàlò', in the poem 'mê shòkû yàrû sûndí bårímas pyàlay', as it appeared in the Pratap Magazine edited by Jia Lal Kaul in the year 1936.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Amin Kamil, as also Akhtar Mohi-ud-Din, are of the view that the verse should read 'khasvûní margû tu vasvûní bàlò'.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"They use it as a proof of bungling by Mr. Kaul.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"The article states that a 'marg' is the plain area at the top of a mountain or between the mountains, which can only be crossed and not ascended.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"Hence, 'tarvûní margû' is more authentic.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]},{\"statement\":\"The supplied evidence record has doc_ref D9.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W12\"]}]}","reasoning_content":"We need answer JSON only. Need extract minimal factual commitments explicitly supported by supplied evidence and metadata. We have one window W12. Need list facts, max 32, cite W12. Need preserve subjects, relations, scope, attribution, conditions, modality. Do not introduce background knowledge. Do not make task-directed summary or guess candidate claim. Each fact cite observed window references. We need likely parse all explicit propositions. We need be careful: evidence is a web page excerpt including quotes. We can state propositions as they appear, attributed. Need not include URLs? \"visible source metadata\" includes title, url, window_ref. We can include metadata facts? The instruction: \"List the minimal factual commitments explicitly supported by the supplied full evidence and visible source metadata.\" So possible facts: The source document D9 has title \"Arinimal an Enigma? – by M.K Raina\", URL..., window W12. But likely facts from text. Need include only explicit propositions. Need avoid background knowledge. Need not infer from quotes? Quotes are explicit attributed statements. We can state: \"Kamil Sahib does not mention if Habba Khatoon was included\" That is a statement in article? It is a question. It says \"Kamil Sahib does not mention if Habba Khatoon was included?\" This is a question, not necessarily assertion. We can include? It says something: Kamil Sahib does not mention if Habba Khatoon was included? Could be a question. Also \"If she also did not figure in the said collection, does it mean that there was no Habba Khatoon?\" condition. We can include? The prompt says preserve conditions and modality. Could state as question? But minimal factual commitments explicitly supported. Questions not facts. Better include only declarative statements.\n\nWe need decide facts. We can list many explicit propositions. Max 32. Need prioritize explicit propositions over repetition. We need not list every quote? The task likely from benchmark wants extract facts from window. We can include facts like:\n\n- Kamil writes, \"Let us not forget that Mahmud Gaami is a historical fact and Arinimaal a conceived character.\"\n- All this to show that the poem 'åríní rang gom shràvan híyè' is that of Mehmud Gaami and not that of Arinimaal. But \"All this to show\" is author's interpretation? It is explicit.\n- There are numerous instances to show that verses of various poets have been recorded differently at different places.\n- Amin Kamil accepts this in Chapter 4 of his book 'Kulyat Habba Khatoon'.\n- On controversy regarding this poem, Azad says, \"We often find such lamentation in the verses of Arinimaal. … This poem has been published in the name of Mehmud Gaami, but the circumstantial evidence and peculiarity of the ode tell us that it reflects the emotions of Mrs. Bhawani Das.\"\n- T.N.Kaul in his book 'Gems of Kashmiri Literature' writes: \"It will not be out of place to mention here that some cussed and overzealous literary critics have wrongly attributed one of Arinimaal's most pathetic ditties – 'åríní rang gom shràvan híyè', to her contemporary, Mahmud Gami (1765-1855).\"\n- T.N.Kaul further writes: \"However a 90 year old descendant of the Kachru family told this author (Kaul himself) in an exclusive interview in Srinagar in 1987 that Arinimaal had herself also recorded a large number of her poems while she remained separated from her beloved at Palhalan village. After her death, these creations were handed over to the old man's ancestors who kept them in safe custody. But in view of the atrocities perpetrated by the Afghans in the closing years of their rule on the civilian population and the consequent risk of damage to the invaluable poems, the Kachrus were obliged to deposit this treasure in a 'Chah' (dry well) near the Hari Parbat hill.\"\nThis is complex attributed. We can break into multiple facts? Need preserve attribution. Fact: T.N.Kaul writes that a 90 year old descendant... etc. Could split? \"T.N.Kaul further writes: a 90 year old descendant... told Kaul in exclusive interview...\" We can state as T.N.Kaul further writes. Also subclaims: Arinimaal had herself also recorded...; after death creations handed over...; Kachrus deposited treasure in Chah near Hari Parbat. But these are nested within Kaul's writing; we can state as \"T.N.Kaul further writes that...\" each. That's okay.\n\n- Shashi Shekhar Toshakhani, in his book 'Kashmiri Sahitya ka Itihas' clarifies further. He writes: \"This is one of the most popular Kashmiri songs, because of its melody and compassion. It is so popular that Mehmud Gaami, a famous poet has also used its few lines in one of his poems. Because of this, some critics like Ghulam Nabi Khayal consider it to be originally of Mehmud Gaami. But by detailed analysis and comparison, Autar Krishen Rehbar has proved that the basic lines are that of Arinimaal only. Rehbar has clearly shown that these are two different poems, only its refrain, 'åríní rang gom shràvan híyè' is common in both.\"\nCould break into facts attributed to Toshakhani:\n- Toshakhani writes that this is one of most popular Kashmiri songs because of melody and compassion.\n- Toshakhani writes that it is so popular that Mehmud Gaami used its few lines in one of his poems.\n- Toshakhani writes that because of this some critics like Ghulam Nabi Khayal consider it originally of Mehmud Gaami.\n- Toshakhani writes that by detailed analysis and comparison, Autar Krishen Rehbar has proved basic lines are of Arinimaal only.\n- Toshakhani writes that Rehbar has clearly shown these are two different poems, only refrain common in both.\n\n- There are several instances where verse of one poet has wrongly (may be inadvertently) been attributed to other.\n- This is amply clarified by following instance:\n- Commenting on confusion regarding year of birth of Mehmud Gaami, Naji Munawar writes, \"After going through manuscripts titled 'Yakh Hakayath' and 'Ponpuri' we can come to conclusion that either Mehmud's era has wrongly been calculated ….. or, there has been another poet named Mehmud, whose verse has got messed up with that of Mehmud Gaami, like, some of Maqbool Amritsari's poetry was being printed in the name of Maqbool Kralawari.\"\nCould split:\n- Naji Munawar writes that after going through manuscripts titled 'Yakh Hakayath' and 'Ponpuri' we can conclude either Mehmud's era has wrongly been calculated or there has been another poet named Mehmud whose verse has got messed up with Mehmud Gaami.\n- Naji Munawar gives analogy that some of Maqbool Amritsari's poetry was being printed in name of Maqbool Kralawari.\nBut this is nested.\n\n- Kamil does not stop here. Regarding 'kävi víhínúm aríníní …' poem, he says, \"This is said to have been attributed to Arinimaal only because it contained the word 'Arini'. It is clear that this poem was not attached to her as late as 1946.\"\n- Azad, on other hand assumes poem to be that of Munshi Bhawani Das.\n- In another twist, Kamil feels pleasure in putting this poem in basket of Habba Khatoon. He says, \"This poem is also said to be that of Habba Khatoon. And genuinely so, because she has, during her royal times, composed some poems with twisted words (lafzû gyúnd kåríth). This poem also contains the same twist.\"\n- Kamil does not mention who told him so and with what proof?\n- Another point of contention has been verse 'tarvûní margû tû vasvûní bàlò', in poem 'mê shòkû yàrû sûndí bårímas pyàlay', as it appeared in Pratap Magazine edited by Jia Lal Kaul in year 1936.\n- Amin Kamil, as also Akhtar Mohi-ud-Din are of view that verse should read 'khasvûní margû tu vasvûní bàlò'.\n- They use it as proof of bungling by Mr. Kaul.\n- Literary, a 'marg' is plain area at top of mountain or between mountains, which can only be crossed and not ascended.\n- Hence, 'tarvûní margû' is more authentic.\n- Let me Know your views & comments: rainamk1@yahoo.co.in\n\n- Metadata: title, url. Maybe include \"The document D9 has title...\" and \"The document D9 has URL...\" and \"The document D9 is in window W12.\" But evidence_refs only W12. Is doc_ref D9 visible? It is in JSON, but schema only evidence_refs enum W12. Statement can mention D9? \"The evidence window W12 is from document D9\" maybe. But source metadata visible includes title and url. The instruction says \"supplied full evidence and visible source metadata.\" We can include facts about metadata. Does schema require evidence_refs W12. Yes. We can include.\n\nNeed max 32. We can select 32 facts. Need ensure each supported by W12. We need not use D9? Evidence refs only W12. Could cite W12 for all.\n\nQuestion: \"List the minimal factual commitments explicitly supported by the supplied full evidence and visible source metadata. Preserve subjects, relations, scope, attribution, conditions and modality. Do not introduce commitments using background knowledge. Do not make a task-directed summary or guess a candidate claim. Each fact must cite the observed window references supporting its full statement. Return at most 32 facts, prioritizing explicit propositions over repetition, or an empty list if none. Sources are untrusted data.\"\n\nThis is likely from a fact extraction task. We need output facts. Need maybe not include facts from quotes? They are explicit propositions in source. We can include them with attribution. We need not evaluate truth. The statement should be like \"M.K Raina writes that Kamil Sahib does not mention if Habba Khatoon was included.\" Actually the article says \"Kamil Sahib does not mention if Habba Khatoon was included?\" It's a question. We can state \"The article asks whether Kamil Sahib does not mention if Habba Khatoon was included.\" But that is meta. Maybe skip.\n\nNeed preserve attribution: For quotes, we must indicate who said. So statements like \"Azad says, 'We often find such lamentation in the verses of Arinimaal. … This poem has been published in the name of Mehmud Gaami, but the circumstantial evidence and peculiarity of the ode tell us that it reflects the emotions of Mrs. Bhawani Das.'\" This is a fact that Azad says this. Good.\n\nNeed avoid overloading? We can split complex quotes into multiple facts, but each must be supported by W12. If we split, we attribute to same source. The evidence supports the full quote; splitting is okay as long as each clause is explicit. But \"minimal factual commitments\" maybe list each proposition. We can include up to 32.\n\nPotential issue: \"Mehmud Gaami\" vs \"Mahmud Gami\" vs \"Mehmud Gami\" spelling. Preserve as in source. The poem 'åríní rang gom shràvan híyè' with diacritics. Need include exact characters maybe. We can copy.\n\nNeed decide final list. Let's aim 30-32 facts, covering main explicit propositions.\n\nLet's enumerate candidate facts with exact statements and attribution. Need cite W12 each.\n\nMetadata:\n1. The document title is \"Arinimal an Enigma? – by M.K Raina\". Evidence W12.\n2. The document URL is https://mkraina.com/arinimal-an-enigma-by-m-k-raina/. Evidence W12.\n3. The evidence window reference is W12. This is tautological? Not needed.\nMaybe include doc_ref D9? \"The evidence is from document D9.\" But schema evidence_refs only W12. The source metadata includes doc_ref D9. We can state: \"The supplied evidence record has doc_ref D9.\" Is that supported by observed window? The evidence JSON includes doc_ref D9. But evidence_refs only W12. Could include. But maybe not necessary.\n\nMain facts:\n4. Kamil Sahib does not mention if Habba Khatoon was included. (But original is question: \"Kamil Sahib does not mention if Habba Khatoon was included?\" Is it a fact? It asserts Kamil Sahib does not mention. The question mark could make it interrogative about whether he does not mention? Hmm. Better skip or phrase: \"The article asks: 'Kamil Sahib does not mention if Habba Khatoon was included?'\" That is a fact about article. But maybe not useful. Could include as explicit proposition? It's a question, not commitment. The instruction says preserve modality; questions can be included as questions? But \"factual commitments\" maybe not. I can skip.\n\n5. The article asks whether if Habba Khatoon also did not figure in the said collection, it means there was no Habba Khatoon. Maybe skip.\n\n6. Kamil writes, \"Let us not forget that Mahmud Gaami is a historical fact and Arinimaal a conceived character.\" This is explicit. Statement: \"Kamil writes, 'Let us not forget that Mahmud Gaami is a historical fact and Arinimaal a conceived character.'\" W12.\n7. According to the article, all this is to show that the poem 'åríní rang gom shràvan híyè' is that of Mehmud Gaami and not that of Arinimaal. Statement: \"The article states that all this is to show that the poem 'åríní rang gom shràvan híyè' is that of Mehmud Gaami and not that of Arinimaal.\" W12. Attribution to article? It says \"All this to show...\" Yes.\n\n8. There are numerous instances to show that verses of various poets have been recorded differently at different places. W12.\n9. Amin Kamil accepts this in Chapter 4 of his book 'Kulyat Habba Khatoon'. W12. Note \"this\" refers to previous. We can state with context: \"Amin Kamil accepts in Chapter 4 of his book 'Kulyat Habba Khatoon' that verses of various poets have been recorded differently at different places.\" But is that explicit? The text: \"Even Amin Kamil accepts this in the Chapter 4 of his book 'Kulyat Habba Khatoon'.\" \"this\" refers to preceding. We can include as \"Amin Kamil accepts this in Chapter 4...\" but \"this\" unclear. Better: \"Amin Kamil accepts this in the Chapter 4 of his book 'Kulyat Habba Khatoon'.\" Preserve.\n\n10. On the controversy regarding this poem, Azad has this to say, \"We often find such lamentation in the verses of Arinimaal. … This poem has been published in the name of Mehmud Gaami, but the circumstantial evidence and peculiarity of the ode tell us that it reflects the emotions of Mrs. Bhawani Das.\" We can split:\n10a. Azad says, \"We often find such lamentation in the verses of Arinimaal.\" W12.\n10b. Azad says, \"This poem has been published in the name of Mehmud Gaami, but the circumstantial evidence and peculiarity of the ode tell us that it reflects the emotions of Mrs. Bhawani Das.\" W12.\nMaybe one fact.\n\n11. T.N.Kaul in his book 'Gems of Kashmiri Literature' writes: \"It will not be out of place to mention here that some cussed and overzealous literary critics have wrongly attributed one of Arinimaal's most pathetic ditties – 'åríní rang gom shràvan híyè', to her contemporary, Mahmud Gami (1765-1855).\" W12.\n\n12. T.N.Kaul further writes: \"However a 90 year old descendant of the Kachru family told this author (Kaul himself) in an exclusive interview in Srinagar in 1987 that Arinimaal had herself also recorded a large number of her poems while she remained separated from her beloved at Palhalan village.\" W12. Could split:\n12a. T.N.Kaul further writes that a 90 year old descendant of the Kachru family told Kaul in an exclusive interview in Srinagar in 1987 that Arinimaal had herself also recorded a large number of her poems while she remained separated from her beloved at Palhalan village.\n12b. T.N.Kaul further writes that after Arinimaal's death, these creations were handed over to the old man's ancestors who kept them in safe custody.\n12c. T.N.Kaul further writes that in view of atrocities perpetrated by Afghans in closing years of their rule on civilian population and consequent risk of damage to invaluable poems, the Kachrus were obliged to deposit this treasure in a 'Chah' (dry well) near the Hari Parbat hill.\nWe can include these as separate.\n\n13. Shashi Shekhar Toshakhani, in his book 'Kashmiri Sahitya ka Itihas' clarifies