s poem was not attached to her as late as 1946.\" W12. Split:\n21a. Regarding 'kävi víhínúm aríníní …' poem, Kamil says it is said to have been attributed to Arinimaal only because it contained the word 'Arini'.\n21b. Kamil says it is clear that this poem was not attached to Arinimaal as late as 1946.\n\n22. Azad, on the other hand assumes the poem to be that of Munshi Bhawani Das. W12.\n\n23. In another twist to the issue, Kamil feels pleasure in putting this poem in the basket of Habba Khatoon. W12. This is article's characterization. We can state \"The article states that in another twist to the issue, Kamil feels pleasure in putting this poem in the basket of Habba Khatoon.\" W12.\n24. Kamil says, \"This poem is also said to be that of Habba Khatoon. And genuinely so, because she has, during her royal times, composed some poems with twisted words (lafzû gyúnd kåríth). This poem also contains the same twist.\" W12. Split:\n24a. Kamil says this poem is also said to be that of Habba Khatoon, and genuinely so, because she has during her royal times composed some poems with twisted words (lafzû gyúnd kåríth).\n24b. Kamil says this poem also contains the same twist.\n25. Kamil does not mention who told him so and with what proof. W12. (article's assertion)\n26. Another point of contention has been the verse 'tarvûní margû tû vasvûní bàlò', in the poem 'mê shòkû yàrû sûndí bårímas pyàlay', as it appeared in the Pratap Magazine edited by Jia Lal Kaul in the year 1936. W12.\n27. Amin Kamil, as also Akhtar Mohi-ud-Din are of the view that the verse should read 'khasvûní margû tu vasvûní bàlò'. W12.\n28. They use it as a proof of bungling by Mr. Kaul. W12.\n29. Literary, a 'marg' is the plain area at the top of a mountain or between the mountains, which can only be crossed and not ascended. W12. (The article states \"Literary\" maybe \"Literally\" typo. We should quote as \"Literary\" or correct? Preserve source: \"Literary, a 'marg' is...\" Could state: \"The article stat