own that these are two different poems, only its refrain, 'åríní rang gom shràvan híyè', is common in both. [W12]\n19. There are several instances where verse of one poet has wrongly (may be inadvertently) been attributed to other. [W12]\n20. Commenting on the confusion regarding year of birth of Mehmud Gaami, Naji Munawar writes, \"After going through the manuscripts titled 'Yakh Hakayath' and 'Ponpuri' we can come to the conclusion that either the Mehmud's era has wrongly been calculated ….. or, there has been another poet named Mehmud, whose verse has got messed up with that of Mehmud Gaami, like, some of Maqbool Amritsari's poetry was being printed in the name of Maqbool Kralawari.\" [W12]\nCould split? Maybe keep as one. We have room.\n\n21. Regarding 'kävi víhínúm aríníní …' poem, Kamil says, \"This is said to have been attributed to Arinimaal only because it contained the word 'Arini'.\" [W12]\n22. Kamil says, \"It is clear that this poem was not attached to her as late as 1946.\" [W12]\n23. Azad, on the other hand, assumes the poem to be that of Munshi Bhawani Das. [W12]\n24. In another twist to the issue, Kamil feels pleasure in putting this poem in the basket of Habba Khatoon. [W12]\n25. Kamil says, \"This poem is also said to be that of Habba Khatoon. And genuinely so, because she has, during her royal times, composed some poems with twisted words (lafzû gyúnd kåríth).\" [W12]\n26. Kamil says, \"This poem also contains the same twist.\" [W12]\n27. Kamil does not mention who told him so and with what proof. [W12]\n28. Another point of contention has been the verse 'tarvûní margû tû vasvûní bàlò', in the poem 'mê shòkû yàrû sûndí bårímas pyàlay', as it appeared in the Pratap Magazine edited by Jia Lal Kaul in the year 1936. [W12]\n29. Amin Kamil, as also Akhtar Mohi-ud-Din, are of the view that the verse should read 'khasvûní margû tu vasvûní bàlò'. [W12]\n30. They use it as a proof of bungling by Mr. Kaul. [W12]\n31. The article states, \"Literary, a 'marg